From b4dc4ebf229aac0c56445150217356936871b2c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 08:45:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 28/32] Fix global kept in a temporary across a loop that needs
 the temporary

SingleBlockLoopOptimisation keeps a global variable that a loop without
calls reads and later stores in the temporary of the stored value: the
load is moved in front of the loop with that temporary as its target,
the store is moved behind the loop.  The temporary must be free to take
the value of the global from the loop entry up to the read.  Two cases
were not checked:

    gv = 5;
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        r += gv;
        gv = n;        // n is needed on entry, the load overwrites it
    }

ended in the assertion !mEntryRequiredTemps[...] (a build without
assertions compares i with the 5 and stores it back), and

    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
    {
        char t = arr[i];
        arr[i] = 9;
        r += gv;       // read as t, the value of this iteration
        gv = t;
    }

compiled without a diagnostic and added arr[i] instead of gv, because
only uses of the temporary in front of the read were excluded, not
assignments.  Both at every optimization level except -O0, native and
bytecode.

The global now stays in memory when the temporary is required on entry
to the loop or referenced in any way in front of the read.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
